WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.264
Environment variables are runtime configuration values available to a
Python process.

2
00:00:05.264 --> 00:00:15.440
They are common in Flask, Django, FastAPI, command-line tools, scripts,
background jobs, data pipelines, notebooks, containers, serverless
functions, and CI/CD jobs.

3
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:21.826
They can select an environment, point to a service, enable a feature, set
a timeout, or provide a credential.

4
00:00:21.826 --> 00:00:28.844
Because they shape application behavior at runtime, they should be treated
as part of the application trust boundary.

5
00:00:28.844 --> 00:00:32.283
Not every environment variable has the same sensitivity.

6
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:38.179
Some values are ordinary configuration, such as a service name, log level,
region, or feature flag.

7
00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:39.933
Some are public identifiers.

8
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:48.285
Others are secrets, such as API keys, database passwords, signing keys,
tokens, private key references, and sensitive connection strings.

9
00:00:48.285 --> 00:00:54.110
A secure design distinguishes those categories instead of treating every
value as harmless text.

10
00:00:54.110 --> 00:01:03.865
Environment variables are convenient because they keep configuration
outside source code and support different deployments from the same
application artifact.

11
00:01:03.865 --> 00:01:06.953
Convenience does not make them automatically safe.

12
00:01:06.953 --> 00:01:19.656
Depending on the platform, environment values can appear in process
environments, debug pages, crash reports, logs, CI output, container
metadata, notebooks, support bundles, child processes, or screenshots.

13
00:01:19.656 --> 00:01:24.989
The exposure path changes, but the principle remains: sensitive values
need protection.

14
00:01:24.989 --> 00:01:35.657
Environment variable security includes loading, validation, exposure
control, logging hygiene, child-process inheritance, rotation, ownership,
and lifecycle management.

15
00:01:35.657 --> 00:01:39.026
The goal is not to avoid environment variables entirely.

16
00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:52.360
The goal is to use them deliberately, minimize secrets, validate critical
values, avoid unsafe defaults, and make configuration predictable,
reviewable, and recoverable across development, deployment, and
operations.
